{"item_id": "feed:https://ruckauthority.com/injuries/rucksack-palsy-numb-hands", "version_id": "33e933df52c538cb87dd722a55fd3dcb7a1d574de307d1729f4a7f4e3232aa85", "decision": "defer", "reason": "Relevance and Credibility: load-carriage nerve risk matters for multi-day carry, but this is mainly medical self-management rather than gear evaluation, and its strap, padding, and hip-belt benefits are editorial cues the article itself says are not clinically validated. Commercial incentives: affiliate commissions and GORUCK-supplied review units apply, and the GR1 padding claim has no independent testing, so defer under Credibility and Unsupported claims until gear-specific independent evidence appears.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/beginners-guide-to-rucking", "version_id": "dbb0e18ebbe81172b08892d816f7c5dc4dd27593c9935d64ada046f1119e6046", "decision": "reject", "reason": "Relevance: this beginner primer for 5 to 10 lb, 20-minute walks is far from Mammoth's multi-day, heavy, team-carried conditions and does not help a competitor decide what to carry. Commercial incentives: the pack and plate mentions carry affiliate commissions and GORUCK-supplied review units, and no independent testing is described, so reject under Relevance and Commercial incentives.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-gear-guide", "version_id": "422a275be764c64e3d288ba08b51fa52cc4b69ec3c6f2835a125f6bbcd8f5ae1", "decision": "defer", "reason": "Relevance and Credibility: packs, plates, shoes, socks, hydration, and watches fall in in-scope categories, but the evidence is mostly manufacturer specifications and retailer-linked recommendations, and claims such as the Darn Tough lifetime guarantee, Hoka Bondi 9 cushioning, and Cordura durability are not tested by independent reviewers here. Commercial incentives: affiliate commissions and GORUCK-supplied review units reduce the weight of its praise, and the source notes flag bulk publishing; the enclosure check cannot be done from this text, so defer under Commercial incentives and Credibility.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://ruckauthority.com/pillars/rucking-injury-prevention", "version_id": "c4217dc00958b969dee299f020f05279a6cb4b2c1689eb0037f74829dfcbc32f", "decision": "defer", "reason": "Relevance and Credibility: the cited military and observational sources address load-carriage injuries relevant to Mammoth, but the central argument is injury prevention and symptom triage rather than gear evaluation, and its gear-specific prevention claims for hip belts, padded straps, and socks rest on military reviews, not tested products. Commercial incentives: affiliate links and GORUCK-supplied units are disclosed and a Garmin product is promoted, so defer under Relevance and Commercial incentives pending gear-specific independent evidence.", "piece_ids": []}
{"item_id": "feed:https://ruckauthority.com/training/is-rucking-bad-for-your-back", "version_id": "6be87287c01a788401892fd4eb4df37a774d39fa1698a9d7ceeebd53c7d6ec5e", "decision": "defer", "reason": "Relevance and Credibility: the article addresses back risk from loaded walking, which bears on multi-day carry, but its central argument is that the evidence is limited and clinical, and its gear references (5.11 RUSH 24 2.0, Mystery Ranch 2 Day Assault hip belt) lack independent test data while the cited lab and small-sample studies do not test long-term injury outcomes. Commercial incentives: affiliate commissions and GORUCK-supplied units are disclosed, so defer under Relevance, Credibility, and Unsupported claims until gear-specific evidence appears.", "piece_ids": []}
